<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wright Flyer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kitty Hawk, Flyer I, 1903 Flyer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wright Flyer is the aircraft that made the first sustained flight by a manned, powered and controlled heavier-than-air machine, on 17 December 1903. Built and flown by Orville and Wilbur Wright, it is a single-place biplane with a 12 horsepower gasoline engine driving two pusher propellers. They flew it four times at what is now Kill Devil Hills, near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina: Orville's first flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 120 feet, and the fourth, by Wilbur, covered 852 feet in 59 seconds before gusts wrecked the machine. After a long dispute between Orville and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smithsonian">Smithsonian</a> Institution it joined the Institution's collection in 1948 and is displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a></p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wright-flyer.jpg" alt="Wright Flyer" width="330" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John T. Daniels · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_flight2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Kill Devil Hills near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, Orville Wright flew the <b>Wright Flyer</b> for 12 seconds and about 37 metres.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
